283
00:15:00,220 --> 00:15:02,760
Maggie. Ignorance is such a terrible burden.

284
00:15:02,760 --> 00:15:07,140
I explained I was using the term ignorant in its classic sense.

285
00:15:09,220 --> 00:15:13,520
Yeah, I know you can't be this upset over the word ignorant, over that word.

286
00:15:13,880 --> 00:15:15,560
I know that you're sublimating.

287
00:15:15,680 --> 00:15:17,020
Tell me what you're sublimating.

288
00:15:17,760 --> 00:15:21,440
Sublimating? Well, I'll tell you what I'm sublimating.

289
00:15:21,840 --> 00:15:26,620
I am sublimating at this very instant a sincere desire to tell you exactly what I

290
00:15:26,620 --> 00:15:29,640
feel about psychiatric terms like sublimating.

291
00:15:31,360 --> 00:15:33,840
All right, I won't use psychiatric terms.

292
00:15:34,160 --> 00:15:35,860
Just tell me what's got you so upset.

293
00:15:36,100 --> 00:15:37,660
You really don't know, do you?

294
00:15:38,060 --> 00:15:39,660
Well, not precisely, no.

295
00:15:39,680 --> 00:15:43,520
Why, it's stunned that you don't know, Jason, since I don't have a thought in my

296
00:15:43,520 --> 00:15:44,800
head that you haven't put there.

297
00:15:44,940 --> 00:15:45,940
That's what I thought.

298
00:15:47,000 --> 00:15:49,560
No, no, no, that's not what I thought.

299
00:15:50,040 --> 00:15:51,440
Well, I'll tell you what I thought.

300
00:15:51,840 --> 00:15:54,860
I thought that you really meant it when you said that the best thing about our

301
00:15:54,860 --> 00:15:59,200
marriage was the fact that we were both individuals with ideas, ambitions, and

302
00:15:59,200 --> 00:16:00,940
identities. Well, you know I do.

303
00:16:01,200 --> 00:16:02,260
I know you did.

304
00:16:02,500 --> 00:16:05,660
Until tonight, I heard you blurt out how you really feel.

305
00:16:05,840 --> 00:16:07,140
I didn't blurt.

306
00:16:10,360 --> 00:16:13,220
So you thought about it before you said it?

307
00:16:13,660 --> 00:16:14,480
Oh, my God.

308
00:16:15,920 --> 00:16:18,060
I was... I always have a...

309
00:16:18,060 --> 00:16:22,660
You've taken a word out of context, and you've blown it out of all proportion.

310
00:16:22,820 --> 00:16:26,200
Oh, there I go, showing my classic ignorance again.

311
00:16:27,600 --> 00:16:29,920
Come on. You know as well as I do.

312
00:16:30,040 --> 00:16:33,220
I love the fact that you have your own ideas, you have your own identity.

313
00:16:33,340 --> 00:16:35,220
I don't even mind a little disagreement, Maggie.

314
00:16:35,400 --> 00:16:36,640
I thrive on it.

315
00:16:37,280 --> 00:16:40,100
Well, Jason, you're in for some real fun.

316
00:16:42,280 --> 00:16:44,180
Okay, now I'm getting angry.

317
00:16:44,260 --> 00:16:45,020
Go with that.

318
00:16:45,140 --> 00:16:46,580
Maggie. Don't supplement.

319
00:16:46,780 --> 00:16:50,300
Come on, you're so upset because this one silly, stupid little remark that

320
00:16:50,300 --> 00:16:51,780
accidentally blurted out...

321
00:16:51,780 --> 00:16:54,560
Ah! So you admit that you blurted?

322
00:16:54,560 --> 00:16:56,060
Oh, are we back to that again?

323
00:16:56,960 --> 00:17:01,120
Jason, you are the psychiatrist, and I'm surprised that you don't know when a person

324
00:17:01,120 --> 00:17:02,480
just blurt out something.

325
