No, no, no, I mean, like, hurt.

352
00:18:47,460 --> 00:18:51,220
Forget it. Wait a second.

353
00:18:51,220 --> 00:18:51,900
Oh, no, I got it.

354
00:18:51,900 --> 00:18:52,380
I got it.

355
00:18:52,380 --> 00:18:53,080
Oh, this is great.

356
00:18:53,140 --> 00:18:54,960
So simple, so perfect, so right.

357
00:18:55,620 --> 00:18:57,800
You know, sometimes my brain frightens me.

358
00:18:59,560 --> 00:19:00,480
All right, now get this.

359
00:19:00,620 --> 00:19:04,440
I'm gonna send Mom a huge bouquet and a wimpy apology from Dad.

360
00:19:04,820 --> 00:19:05,880
That's your idea?

361
00:19:06,900 --> 00:19:09,180
Yeah. Mike, it'll never work.

362
00:19:09,600 --> 00:19:11,020
Well, did on the Cosby show.

363
00:19:14,320 --> 00:19:16,460
That's TV. This is real life.

364
00:19:18,040 --> 00:19:20,820
Hold it. You're saying the cosplay show isn't real?

365
00:19:23,800 --> 00:19:28,020
Oh, Mike, how do you know it's Dad who should apologize to Mom?

366
00:19:28,880 --> 00:19:33,080
Well, Carol, whenever a man and a woman fight, every guy on the planet knows who has

367
00:19:33,080 --> 00:19:40,940
to apologize. Honey?

368
00:19:44,940 --> 00:19:50,380
Honey? Honey? Honey!

369
00:19:52,460 --> 00:19:58,660
What? Well, I was wondering if maybe I could just have a word with you before you

370
00:19:58,660 --> 00:19:59,220
went to work.

371
00:20:00,120 --> 00:20:02,040
Well, I'm running kind of late this morning, Jason.

372
00:20:02,120 --> 00:20:03,480
I'm interviewing Governor Cuomo.

373
00:20:03,920 --> 00:20:04,820
How about tonight?

374
00:20:04,820 --> 00:20:09,060
Well, I was hoping we could, you know, talk just before you left, because I've been

375
00:20:09,060 --> 00:20:10,460
thinking about the way things

376
00:20:11,120 --> 00:20:14,780
sometimes get out of hand, and events gain a momentum of their own.

377
00:20:14,800 --> 00:20:18,160
And, uh, then we end up saying things we don't mean.

378
00:20:18,820 --> 00:20:20,020
I know... Gotta go.

379
00:20:26,560 --> 00:20:27,380
Thank you. Thank you.

380
00:20:27,540 --> 00:20:28,800
Thank you. Hey, hold on a second.

381
00:20:28,860 --> 00:20:30,780
I can't. My interview's in half an hour.

382
00:20:31,180 --> 00:20:32,640
Well, don't you think you should dress for it?

383
00:20:32,640 --> 00:20:35,180
Jason, I don't need a fashion critique.

384
00:20:35,340 --> 00:20:38,520
I hardly slept a wink last night, and I'm not thinking very clearly this morning.

385
00:20:39,260 --> 00:20:40,800
Well, this is beyond fashion.

386
00:20:42,340 --> 00:20:46,120
I mean, you approach the governor dressed like that, and his bodyguards are gonna

387
00:20:46,120 --> 00:20:47,200
wrestle you to the ground.

388
00:20:48,380 --> 00:20:50,980
Oh, so what do you wear when you interview the governor?

389
00:20:51,240 --> 00:20:53,840
Well, come on, I'm just saying this is a respected public official.

390
00:20:54,240 --> 00:20:56,420
This is Mario Cuomo, not Bozo Cuomo.

391
00:20:57,320 --> 00:20:59,600
So you're saying the clothing has something to do with character?

392
00:20:59,600 --> 00:21:00,380
Oh, of course.

393
00:21:00,900 --> 00:21:03,580
No! No, no, no, no.

394
00:21:03,620 --> 00:21:04,960
Too late, Jason.

395
00:21:05,020 --> 00:21:05,660
You said it.

396
00:21:05,700 --> 00:21:06,840
You feel the same way I do.

397
00:21:06,880 --> 00:21:08,220
I don't. You do.

398
00:21:08,280 --> 00:21:09,200
No, I don't.

399
00:21:09,220 --> 00:21:12,740
Just because you feel one way about certain things doesn't mean I have to feel the

400
00:21:12,740 --> 00:21:14,780
same way. Exactly.

401
00:21:15,540 --> 00:21:18,140
That's exactly the point I've been trying to make.

402
00:21:19,540 --> 00:21:20,560
But you tricked me.

403
00:21:22,940 --> 00:21:27,020
Honey, I wouldn't have stooped so low if I didn't love you.

404
00:21:27,400 --> 00:21:29,180
So I deserve to be tricked?

405
00:21:31,680 --> 00:21:33,740
I guess I do.

406
00:21:34,820 --> 00:21:37,300
I'm really sorry about what I said last night.

407
00:21:38,340 --> 00:21:41,040
Honey, you embarrassed me in front of all those people.

408
00:21:43,440 --> 00:21:46,420
I'm sorry. I was ignorant.

409
00:21:51,400 --> 00:21:52,920
But just for the record,

410
00:21:53,680 --> 00:21:56,000
I still don't believe in dress codes at school.

411
00:21:56,460 --> 00:21:59,200
And just for the record, I'm still for them.

412
00:21:59,200 --> 00:22:03,280
So we can either debate about it or we...

413
00:22:15,260 --> 00:22:21,140
Excuse me. Dr.

414
00:22:21,340 --> 00:22:23,800
Jason Siever? Oh, honey.

415
00:22:24,180 --> 00:22:25,300
You shouldn't have.

416
00:22:25,300 --> 00:22:26,240
That's my job.

417
00:22:29,420 --> 00:22:34,200
Oh. Jason, I love you even when you think you don't agree with me.

418
00:22:34,220 --> 00:22:38,200
Love, Maggie. Aw, honey, that's so sweet.

419
00:22:38,360 --> 00:22:39,480
Well, I just thought that after...

420
00:22:39,480 --> 00:22:41,400
I wish I had been, you know, my...

421
00:22:41,400 --> 00:22:44,080
More flowers. These are for Maggie Siever.

422
00:22:44,420 --> 00:22:49,540
Oh. And, hey, just seeing people like you is tipping up for me.

423
00:22:51,440 --> 00:22:58,360
Yeah. Jason, how sweet.

424
00:22:58,720 --> 00:22:59,720
Well, I, uh...

425
00:22:59,720 --> 00:23:02,920
Maggie, dearest, how could I have been so wrong?

426
00:23:03,100 --> 00:23:04,300
I'm a complete wimp.

427
00:23:04,520 --> 00:23:10,380
Love, Jason. Oh, honey, don't you think you're being a little hard on yourself?

428
00:23:11,920 --> 00:23:12,480
Nah.
